       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 2000 mg three times daily comes to 6,000 mg/day, which is well above the usual maximum for acetaminophen in an adult:\n\n- **Daily total:** The maximum is 4,000 mg/day. Many institutions cap it at 3,000 mg/day. 6,000 mg/day carries a serious risk of hepatotoxicity, even with normal liver function.\n- **Single dose:** The usual maximum is 1,000 mg per dose. A 2,000 mg dose is double that.\n\nHere are some options:\n1. **1,000 mg PO three times daily.** This is 3,000 mg/day.\n2. **1,000 mg PO four times daily.** This is 4,000 mg/day, the maximum.\n3. **650 mg PO every 6 hours.** This is 2,600 mg/day.\n\nIf you meant a different dose or frequency, such as 1,000 mg or 500 mg, tell me which and I'll enter it. If you do want 2,000 mg three times daily, please confirm that explicitly and give the clinical rationale. I can add that to the order note, though pharmacy will probably flag it."
      }
